<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>biotechnology-principles</title></head><body><h1>biotechnology-principles</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Principles of biotechnology</li></ul><h2>Principles of biotechnology</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>### 1. What is Biotechnology?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Biotechnology is the use of living organisms or their products to make or modify products for practical use. Modern biotechnology involves genetic engineering.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>### 2. Core Techniques</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>- **Recombinant DNA (rDNA) Technology**: Combining DNA from different sources</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>- **PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction)**: Amplifying DNA segments</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>- **Gel Electrophoresis**: Separating DNA fragments by size</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>- **Gene Cloning**: Making copies of genes</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>### 3. Tools of Recombinant DNA Technology</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>- **Restriction Enzymes (Molecular Scissors)**: Cut DNA at specific sequences (palindromes)</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>- **Examples**: EcoRI (cuts at GAATTC), HindIII (cuts at AAGCTT)</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>- **Sticky ends**: Overhanging single-stranded ends after cutting</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>- **Blunt ends**: No overhang after cutting</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>- **Vectors (Molecular Vehicles)**: Carry foreign DNA into host</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>- **Examples**: Plasmids, bacteriophages, cosmids, Ti plasmid</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>- **Competent Host**: Modified to accept foreign DNA (CaCl2 treatment for bacteria)</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>### 4. Steps in rDNA Technology</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>1. Isolation of genetic material (DNA extraction)</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>2. Cutting DNA with restriction enzymes</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>3. Separation by gel electrophoresis</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>4. Insertion of DNA into vector</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>5. Transformation into host cell</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>6. Expression of desired gene</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>### 5. PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction)</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>- Amplifies specific DNA segment millions of times</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>- **Steps**: Denaturation (94°C), Annealing (50-65°C), Extension (72°C)</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>- **Enzyme**: Taq polymerase (heat-stable from Thermus aquaticus)</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>- **Components**: Template DNA, primers, dNTPs, Taq polymerase</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>- **Applications**: Forensics, disease diagnosis, genetic research</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>### 6. Gel Electrophoresis</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>- Separates DNA fragments by size using electric field</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>- DNA is negatively charged, moves toward positive electrode</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>- Smaller fragments move faster through agarose gel</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>- Visualized with ethidium bromide under UV light</td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td>### 7. Entrance Exam Focus</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td>Identify restriction enzymes; Describe PCR steps; Explain vector requirements.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>DNA ligase seals the sugar-phosphate backbone after complementary sticky ends pair, forming the recombinant plasmid.</td></tr><tr><td>EcoRI cuts 5'-GAATTC-3' to leave 5' AATT overhangs, and HindIII cuts 5'-AAGCTT-3' to leave 5' AGCT overhangs.</td></tr><tr><td>The rDNA steps are: isolation of DNA, cutting with restriction enzymes, separation by gel electrophoresis, insertion into a vector, transformation of the host, and expression of the gene.</td></tr><tr><td>PCR components are template DNA, forward and reverse primers, the four dNTPs, Taq polymerase and a buffer containing Mg2+ ions.</td></tr><tr><td>Bands are visualised after staining with ethidium bromide under UV light; smaller fragments travel furthest from the wells.</td></tr><tr><td>Gene cloning in a vector produces many identical copies, that is clones, of a single DNA fragment.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>x Any enzyme can cut DNA. x Only specific restriction enzymes recognize specific sequences.</td></tr><tr><td>x PCR makes infinite copies. x Each cycle doubles DNA - limited by reagents.</td></tr><tr><td>x DNA moves to positive electrode because it's positive. x DNA is NEGATIVE, moves to POSITIVE.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Biotechnology is the use of living organisms or their products to make or modify products for practical use, and modern biotechnology is built on recombinant DNA (rDNA) technology. The essential tools are restriction enzymes, which cut DNA at specific palindromic recognition sequences such as EcoRI at GAATTC to give sticky or blunt ends, and vectors such as plasmids, bacteriophages and cosmids, which carry foreign DNA into a competent host. rDNA technology proceeds through six steps from isolation of DNA to expression of the gene, while PCR amplifies a chosen DNA segment using thermostable Taq polymerase and gel electrophoresis separates fragments according to size. These techniques together underpin every product of modern biotechnology.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Principles of biotechnology</td><td>Biotechnology is the use of living organisms or their products to make or modify products for practical use, and modern biotechnology is built on recombinant DNA (rDNA) technology. The essential tools are restriction enzymes, which cut DNA at specific palindromic recognition sequences such as EcoRI at GAATTC to give sticky or blunt ends, and vectors such as plasmids, bacteriophages and cosmids, which carry foreign DNA into a competent host. rDNA technology proceeds through six steps from isolation of DNA to expression of the gene, while PCR amplifies a chosen DNA segment using thermostable Taq polymerase and gel electrophoresis separates fragments according to size. These techniques together underpin every product of modern biotechnology.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Biotechnology Principles.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>PCR amplifies a selected DNA region and gel electrophoresis separates fragments by size. These tools support research and diagnosis, but results depend on controls and the specific question asked.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>Detection of genetic material alone does not necessarily show that an organism is alive or caused symptoms. For genome editing, consider off-target changes, mosaicism, delivery to the intended cells and the distinction between somatic and heritable changes.</p><h3>Open research question — Precision and safety in genome editing</h3><p>Researchers continue to improve how genome edits are delivered and assessed. Off-target changes, mosaicism, intended tissue, and—in heritable editing—effects on future generations are distinct scientific and ethical considerations.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>For “Biotechnology Principles”, identify one assumption in the model, explain what observation would test it, and state what evidence could distinguish the accepted account from the open question “Precision and safety in genome editing”.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/17-1-biotechnology">OpenStax Biology: biotechnology</a></li><li><a href="https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/policy-issues/Genome-Editing/ethical-concerns">NHGRI: ethical concerns in genome editing</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>